\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Transductive versus semi-inductive link prediction. In both panels, the dashed edge marks the relation to be predicted. (a) In the transductive setting, the incident $q$ whose causer relation is masked was already present during training, so an entity-specific representation for $q$ has already been learned. (b) In the semi-inductive setting, the new incident $q_{\text  {new}}$ did not exist during training; the model must instead use its known connection to the material $m$ to judge the plausibility of the candidate triple $(q_{\text  {new}}, \text  {CAUSED\_BY}, c_1)$ relating it to a causer.}}{21}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:transductive_inductive}{{2.3}{21}{Transductive versus semi-inductive link prediction. In both panels, the dashed edge marks the relation to be predicted. (a) In the transductive setting, the incident $q$ whose causer relation is masked was already present during training, so an entity-specific representation for $q$ has already been learned. (b) In the semi-inductive setting, the new incident $q_{\text {new}}$ did not exist during training; the model must instead use its known connection to the material $m$ to judge the plausibility of the candidate triple $(q_{\text {new}}, \text {CAUSED\_BY}, c_1)$ relating it to a causer}{figure.caption.14}{}}
